.Dd August 24, 2026
.Dt SOWA-LICENSE 8
.Os Sowa
.Sh NAME
.Nm sowa-license
.Nd what this system is licensed under, and by whom
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -root Ar dir
.Op Ar command
.Op Ar package ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Sowa is a few thousand lines of its own code wrapped around some eighty
upstream projects.
Four packages are Sowa's own work -
.Sy sowa-init
,
.Sy sowa-release
,
.Sy custom-installers
and the optional
.Sy sowa-monitor
- and all are GPL-3.0-or-later, as is the build system that produced the
image.
Everything else belongs to the people who wrote it and is shipped under their
terms, from unmodified sources.
.Nm
is how a machine says which is which, without anyone having to go and read a
table somewhere else.
.Pp
It reads three things, all of them local:
.Bl -tag -width "/usr/share/licenses/<package>" -offset indent
.It Pa /var/lib/sowa/db/<package>/desc
the licence and copyright of every installed package, written when the package
was installed.
.It Pa /var/lib/sowa/repo/index
the same fields for packages the repository offers and this system does not
have, if an index has been fetched.
.It Pa /usr/share/licenses/<package>/
the licence texts themselves, which every package carries as files it owns.
.El
.Pp
Nothing here contacts the network and nothing here needs root: what a system is
licensed under is something it should be able to state about itself while
offline and while logged in as anybody.
.Pp
With no arguments at all,
.Cm summary
is what runs.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width "list --available"
.It Cm summary
What this system as a whole is licensed under: how many packages are Sowa's own
work and how many are other people's, which packages Sowa wrote, and a count of
every licence identifier in use across the installed system.  It also counts
the licence classifications of installed packages.
.It Cm list
Every installed package with its version, whose copyright it is, and its
licence, and its licence classification.
.It Cm list Fl -available
The same for packages the repository offers and this system does not have.
Needs an index, so
.Ic sowa-pkg update
first.
.It Cm list Fl -all
Both, in one table.
.It Cm show Ar package ...
Print the licence texts a package carries, in full, through a pager when there
is somebody to read them.
.It Cm files Ar package ...
List the licence files a package carries, without printing them.
.It Cm sowa
The parts of this system Sowa itself wrote.
.It Ar package
A bare package name is the same as
.Cm show Ar package ,
because it is the question people actually ask.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "--root dir"
.It Fl -root Ar dir
Ask about another root filesystem: a disk mounted somewhere, or an image being
assembled.
The database, the index and the licence texts are all taken from under that
directory.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print usage and exit.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/var/lib/sowa/repo/index" -compact
.It Pa /usr/share/licenses
one directory per package, holding the texts it ships.
They are files the package owns, so they are listed by
.Ic sowa-pkg files ,
checked by
.Ic sowa-pkg verify ,
and removed with the package.
.It Pa /var/lib/sowa/db
the installed package database.
.It Pa /var/lib/sowa/repo/index
the verified repository index.
.It Pa /etc/os-release
where the distribution's name and version are read from.
It is read rather than sourced: the file is shell-shaped by specification, but
sourcing one to learn a name runs whatever is in it.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
A root filesystem with no package database in it is an error, since the answer
would otherwise be an empty and confident one.
.Sh EXAMPLES
What the machine is, in one screen:
.Pp
.Dl sowa-license
.Pp
The licence of everything installed, and of everything on offer:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sowa-license list
sowa-license list --all
.Ed
.Pp
The full text of what a package ships:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sowa-license show gcc
sowa-license gcc          # the same thing
sowa-license files gcc    # just the paths
.Ed
.Pp
About a disk mounted elsewhere:
.Pp
.Dl sowa-license --root /mnt list
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sowa-pkg 8
.Sh CAVEATS
A licence identifier is what the package's own metadata records, and an
expression joined with
.Sy AND
means every one of the identifiers applies, to different files.
The counts in
.Cm summary
are therefore per identifier: a package under several licences appears under
each of them.
.Pp
A package that records no licence at all is counted and reported as such.
That is a packaging bug rather than a package without a licence.
.Pp
The classification is local and is based on the SPDX identifiers recorded in
the package metadata.  It is one of
.Sy osi-approved
(every applicable licence is on the OSI Approved Licences list),
.Sy foss
(free and open source, but not OSI-approved),
.Sy foss-mixed
(OSI-approved and other FOSS licences both apply), or
.Sy source-available-non-free
(source-available terms that are not free).  An unreviewed identifier is
reported conservatively as
.Sy source-available-non-free .
